<h2>A card swap, a conviction, and its foundations</h2>
<p>The prosecution alleged the classic ATM-kiosk deception: the complainant&rsquo;s debit card swapped in a moment of engineered confusion, followed by withdrawals totalling Rs 65,300. Two courts convicted. The revision succeeded because, examined link by link, the chain had never been forged in admissible form.</p>

<h2>Five failures, catalogued</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Recoveries legally non est.</strong> No Malkhana register was produced; the seizures were unsealed; the seizure box was manufactured a month <em>after</em> the recorded seizure date; and only 9 of the 26 ATM cards allegedly seized were produced in court. Recoveries so documented prove nothing.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Electronic records without certificates.</strong> The bank printout, tax invoice and merchant slip came without Section 65B Evidence Act and Bankers&rsquo; Books Evidence Act certificates. Applying <em>Anvar P.V. v. P.K. Basheer</em>, they were inadmissible — and exhibit-marking at trial cured nothing.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. A tainted TIP.</strong> The accused were shown to the complainant at the police station before the parade, and the Magistrate&rsquo;s own testimony contradicted the TIP record. Identification evidence of that kind carries no weight.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. Parity violated.</strong> The trial court acquitted a co-accused by disbelieving the recovery evidence — then convicted the petitioners on the identical material. Article 14 does not permit the same evidence to be true for one accused and false for another, as <em>Javed Shaukat Ali Qureshi</em> holds.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>5. Antecedents as proof.</strong> Criminal antecedents were used as substantive evidence of guilt, impermissibly shifting the burden onto the accused.</div>
</div>

<h2>The revisional threshold, honestly applied</h2>
<p>The judgment is candid about the jurisdictional tension. Revision is supervisory; concurrent findings of fact ordinarily end the matter. But the supervisory power exists precisely for the case where the findings rest on no admissible evidence — where what the courts below called appreciation was in truth assumption. Citing the line that includes <em>Sujit Biswas</em> and <em>Sanjaysinh Ramrao Chavan</em>, the Court located perversity not in a different view of credible evidence, but in the absence of legally cognisable evidence to view at all.</p>

<h2>Lessons for cyber-fraud litigation</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For investigators.</strong> Digital-finance prosecutions live and die on custody discipline: sealed seizures, contemporaneous registers, and certificates under Section 65B — now Section 63 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam — obtained when the records are extracted, not at trial.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For defence counsel.</strong> Audit the exhibit list against the certificates before cross-examination begins; an admissibility objection preserved on the record is worth more than any amount of credibility attack later.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For appellate strategy.</strong> Where both courts below convicted, frame the challenge as absence of admissible evidence, not as a plea to re-weigh — the former opens the revisional door that the latter keeps shut.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For parity arguments.</strong> Map each co-accused&rsquo;s treatment against the evidence relied on; an acquittal next door on identical material is among the strongest revisional grounds available.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">The acquittal was on benefit of doubt, with fines ordered refunded. The decision does not hold that no fraud occurred — it holds that the State never proved one by legal means. The distinction is the entire discipline of criminal evidence.</div>
